levels_of_assessment.quote:
Building Work is Accepted Development, unless:
a Category of Development and Assessment Table states otherwise;
the work includes the construction of work identified in the Local Government Infrastructure Plan;
the work includes the undertaking of work on land owned or controlled by the Local Government (including by way of trust or easement);
the work includes the undertaking of work otherwise dedicated and / or intended to be maintained by the Local Government; or
the works are otherwise prescribed in the Act or the Regulation.

definitions.text:
Site cover, of development, means the portion of the site, expressed as a percentage, that will be covered by a building or structure, measured to its outermost projection, after the development is carried out, other than a building or structure, or part of a building or structure, that is:
in a landscaped or open space area, including, for example, a gazebo or shade structure;
a basement that is completely below ground level and used for car parking;
the eaves of a building; or
a sun shade.

Gross floor area (GFA), for a building, means the total floor area of all storeys of the building, measured from the outside of the external walls and the centre of any common walls of the building, other than areas used for:
building services, plant or equipment;
access between levels;
a ground floor public lobby;
a mall;
parking, loading or manoeuvring vehicles; or
unenclosed private balconies, whether roofed or not.

rules.verbatim_text:
RO1.2
Open Class 10 buildings and structures, including car accommodation may also be located to the side of the main building in Area B (refer to Figure 8.2.3.3.20.1) where taking a form that:
is detached from the main building;
is open or battened where facing the street or public realm;
is in alignment with or set back behind the line of the internal frontage verandah wall;
is a maximum of 6 metres wide;
where including a roof:
has a roof pitch which is a minimum of 15 degrees;
has a gable or hip roof form;
has roof overhangs that are a minimum of 300mm on all sides;
contains corrugated metal roofing material or matches the roofing material used on the main building; and
has a quad gutter profile or matches the gutter profile used on the main building;
has 100mm x 100mm support posts (timber or steel); and
where enclosed:
with timber battens, maintaining a minimum 30% transparency by providing spacing between each batten;
doors facing the street (swing, panel lift or tilt) maintain a minimum 30% transparency by providing spacing between each batten; and
enclosed spaces are located at the rear of the building or structure (refer to Figure 8.2.3.3.20.2, Figure K) and use horizontal cladding to match existing buildings on site.

RO1.3
Where the side setbacks are less than 3 metres and access to the side or rear of an existing dwelling is not possible due to existing dwelling placement, a single carport can be erected between the dwelling and the primary street alignment in Area C in a location that minimises obscuring the view of the character dwelling (refer to Figure 8.2.3.3.20.1) where taking a form that:
is detached from the main building;
is a maximum of 3.6 metres wide and 6 metres long;
has a roof pitch which matches the main building’s predominant roof pitch;
has roof overhangs on all sides that are a minimum of 300mm;
has a gable or hip roof form;
has 100mm x 100mm support posts (timber or steel);
corrugated metal roofing material or matches the roofing material used on the main building;
has a quad gutter profile or matches the gutter profile used on the main building; and
the carport may be enclosed using:
timber battens, maintaining a minimum 30% transparency by providing spacing between each batten; and
doors facing the street (swing, panel lift or tilt) maintain a minimum 30% transparency by providing spacing between each timber batten.

RO1.4
On a secondary frontage of a corner site (refer to Area D on Figure 8.2.3.3.20.1) open car accommodation may also be located where taking a form that:
is detached from the main building;
is a single or double width carport detached from the main building;
is a single gable for single carports;
for double carports, provide a pair of gabled or hipped roofs of equal width facing the secondary frontage (refer Figure 8.2.3.3.20.2, Figures B, F & G);
is a maximum of 6 metres wide and 6 metres long;
has a roof pitch which matches the main building’s predominant roof pitch;
provides roof overhangs on all sides that are a minimum of 300mm;
has 100mm x 100mm support posts (timber or steel);
provides corrugated metal roofing material or matches the roofing material used on the main building; and
has a quad gutter profile or matches the gutter profile used on the main building.

PO7.2
New buildings or extensions to existing buildings contribute to the rhythm of the siting and spacing of buildings in the street, particularly where the siting and spacing pattern is highly regular.
AO7.2
New buildings are setback from the street an equal or greater distance than adjacent character buildings;
New buildings maintain a consistent spacing and harmonious rhythm (refer to Figure 8.2.3.3.3.3);
Where there is no character building or character place for setback reference, or a discordant rhythm exists new buildings are setback to comply with the specific outcomes for each zone;
New buildings or extensions to existing buildings are not constructed in front of the building alignment of existing character buildings;
New buildings, or extensions to existing buildings, ensure the distance between the boundary and the side external wall reflect the ratio of built form to open space which is similar to other character buildings in the street; and
New buildings are built to the property boundary where:
adjoining character buildings are constructed to the property boundary; and
the new building complies with the performance outcomes for the relevant zones.

PO5.1
Where the site is situated within a character area or residential setting, buildings are in the range of one to two storeys in height (including single storey high set) with an emphasis placed on elevated buildings on stumps, consistent with the height of adjoining character buildings or the predominant height or rhythm of character buildings within the streetscape or hillscape;
Where the site is situated within a centre or industrial zone or a commercial or industrial setting, buildings are in the range of one to two storeys in height, consistent with the height of adjacent character buildings or the predominant height or rhythm of character buildings within the streetscape or hillscape;
An additional storey is not provided unless appropriate with regard to:
the character and amenity of the area and the overall townscape including hillscapes;
the scale of adjoining development and the predominant height of buildings within the street or hillscape;
the retention of important view corridors; and
the retention of the significance of landmark sites and structures;
Where the site is situated within a commercial or industrial zone, or a commercial or industrial setting, the height of buildings at the street alignment are designed to:
maintain a human scale, particularly at the street level; and
maintain the visual prominence of significant landscape features (including landmark sites) and conserve desirable view corridors; and
New buildings which are situated adjacent to character buildings or character places are designed to ensure that the building is not of a height or bulk to:
visually dominate the character building or character place; or
obstruct views of the character building or character place where visible from a road or the public realm.
AO5.1
No accepted outcome provided.
